From 86b225c755d5ee4207e97325a7e6e8975359fecf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 20:15:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(nav): the geography page was not in the navigation, and
 three guards were reading the wrong lists
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

WHAT THE EXISTING GUARDS WERE NOT SAYING

verify-navigation proved every route PATTERN is within three clicks of the
homepage. `/service-areas/[city]` counts once there, so the check is satisfied
by a single city being linked while eighty-eight are not — and the same holds
for forty-four glossary terms, sixteen guides and eleven corridors. Nine new
tests measure the real thing: sitemap coverage against every indexable page,
count parity between each index and the sitemap for its family, and whether any
page is a dead end.

FOUR REAL FAULTS

  · /service-areas was in the footer and NOT in the header. The page a
    "hardwood flooring near me" search lands on, the payoff of the whole
    corridor model, and the primary navigation did not mention it. There is now
    a "Where we work" group, with both of its counts derived.

  · The /service-areas H1 read "Hardwood flooring across the GTA" above a list
    of eighty-nine places, twenty-six of them in New York State. The most
    important geography page on the site was contradicting its own contents in
    the one line a search engine quotes.

  · The navigation said "Nine routes, and what coverage means" for as long as
    there had been eleven corridors, and /api/v1/corridors said the same in its
    manifest summary.

  · /products/floorforge was the only dead end on the site: two links, both
    above the fold, on a page a robotics search lands on. The flooring business
    is the reason anyone trusts the robot, so it now names the flooring
    business — where Ecowoods works, the machines, the framework, the papers.

THREE READERS THAT HAD GONE BLIND

verify-destinations built its service-area manifest by scanning lib/seo-data.ts
for `slug: 'x'` literals. There are none: those slugs are DERIVED from three
lists of names. It had been returning whatever `slug:` literals happened to be
in the file for other reasons, and nobody found out until the first link to a
city page from a scanned component reported three published municipalities as
404s. It now derives the same set the site does, and refuses to run if it comes
back smaller than CITY_CONTENT.

verify-agentic parsed ENDPOINTS with a span that could not cross a closing
brace, so a summary becoming a template literal made one route look undeclared.
It now allows `${…}` and checks parsed entries against declared entries.

verify-geo-green looked for two specific numbers beside the word "areas", which
is why "Nine routes" sat in the navigation unnoticed. Any written-out number
before a territory noun now fails — on the same line only, after it matched a
case-study slug ending "-three-level-transition" against a `routes:` field
below it. lib/changelog.ts is exempt by name: a dated record is the one place a
stale count is correct, and rewriting it would falsify the history it keeps.

TWO SMALLER THINGS

The desktop mega panel now has a visible ✕. Escape closed it and outside-click
closed it, and neither is discoverable — on a touchscreen neither is even
available, leaving the trigger as the only way out, which is a thing people have
to be taught. The mobile drawer has had one all along.

CoverageMap is deleted. A GTA-only drawing with "sixteen municipalities" in its
aria-label, kept alive only by the component rendering it being commented out on
the homepage. SpecsCoverage now renders TerritoryMap.

66 guards green, 332 tests, tsc at its 115-error baseline.

